# Text is stored and checked out with one line ending on every platform.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf rewrites source files on the way out of the
# repository. The tests that assert on file content then measure the checkout rather than the
# code: a block editor that preserves a file's existing line endings faithfully reproduced CRLF
# and was told it had produced a string one byte too long, on CI only, for a reason nothing in
# the test mentioned.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF wherever they are checked out: a CR at the end of the shebang makes
# the kernel look for an interpreter that does not exist.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows tooling that is happier with CRLF.
*.sln text eol=crlf
*.iss text eol=crlf

# Left exactly as committed.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.dmg binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.ppk binary
